    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that formula makes sense. but the variability part is the hardest to pin down because sometimes the delays are just... random. like, we'll have a month where everything is fine and then suddenly a shipment just disappears or gets stuck for three weeks for no reason. how do i build a buffer for those weird, sudden disruptions that don't seem to follow a pattern?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For unpredictable \"black swan\" events like stuck shipments, standard statistical formulas often fall short, so you might want to implement a hard buffer based on your worst-case historical scenario (e.g., 2-3 weeks of extra stock) plus a high-value critical component reserve for high-demand items. Since you can't model randomness with precision, consider manually adding extra days to your reorder point during peak transit seasons or specific holidays when air freight is unreliable. It is also wise to diversify your suppliers from Shenzhen to build resilience outside the supply chain entirely.",
      "source": "target",
